<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.barezen</groupId>
    <artifactId>barezen-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>barezen-http-jdk</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>BareZen HTTP Adapter: JDK HttpServer</name>
  <description>
    The first implementation of the barezen-web server contract, built on com.sun.net.httpserver. Owns a bound port,
    an executor and a thread provider; translates an HttpExchange into a Request, dispatches it through the Router,
    and translates the Response back. Contains no routing rules, no middleware, no error format beyond the minimal
    one the contract cannot express, and no HTTP parser of its own.
  </description>

  <!--
    Deliberately no <maven.compiler.release> override here. com.sun.net.httpserver is a JDK 11 API and the
    adapter uses nothing newer, so this module is the first one that could have tempted an author to raise the
    baseline for no reason at all. See ADR-0002 and ADR-0021.
  -->

  <!--
    Three compile dependencies, and the shape of them is the phase's claim: web (the contract it implements),
    core (ThreadProvider, which it borrows or creates), slf4j-api (a handler failure has to be recorded somewhere,
    and ADR-0001 already chose the facade). Nothing else reaches this module — no Netty, no Jetty, no Servlet, no
    JSON library — because an adapter that needed them would stop being replaceable, which is the only reason the
    contract exists (ADR-0018 §12, ADR-0021).

    The direction is one way. barezen-web declares no dependency on this module and barezen-core declares none on
    either, so an application that wants a different server deletes this artifact from its build and changes one
    string. The rules that make that mechanical are in BarezenHttpJdkArchitectureTest, which is the first test in
    the reactor that imports all three modules' classes at once.

    No logback in the compile scope, for the same reason barezen-web has none: the production code depends on the
    SLF4J facade only (ADR-0001). Test scope is the exception, and it is a real one rather than a preference -
    ADR-0023 §4 says a handler failure goes to the log and not to the socket, and the socket half of that claim is
    a response-body assertion while the log half needs a binding to observe at all. Without one SLF4J silently
    discards every event, so a test could not tell "recorded" from "swallowed". barezen-core already carries
    logback as a test dependency for exactly this reason; the shipped jar still has no implementation in it.
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.barezen</groupId>
      <artifactId>barezen-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.barezen</groupId>
      <artifactId>barezen-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
      <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
      <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>ch.qos.logback</groupId>
      <artifactId>logback-classic</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>prepare-agent</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
          <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <profiles>
    <profile>
      <!--
        Same gate and same JDK 21 binding as barezen-core and barezen-web (ADR-0002). Unlike them, this module has
        one genuinely platform-conditional line: the thread provider it creates resolves to virtual threads on 21
        and platform threads on 17, and the JDK's own executor plumbing is identical either way. So there is no
        unreachable half of the bundle here, and no reason for a lower bar on 17 — 17 verifies compatibility, 21
        verifies compatibility and quality.
      -->
      <id>coverage-gate</id>
      <activation>
        <jdk>[21,)</jdk>
      </activation>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
            <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <id>check-coverage</id>
                <phase>verify</phase>
                <goals>
                  <goal>check</goal>
                </goals>
                <configuration>
                  <rules>
                    <rule>
                      <element>BUNDLE</element>
                      <limits>
                        <limit>
                          <counter>LINE</counter>
                          <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                          <minimum>0.85</minimum>
                        </limit>
                      </limits>
                    </rule>
                  </rules>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
